====Parliamentary republics with directly elected executive presidency====
Presidency is directly elected by the voters (from among candidates nominated by the legislature in the case of Kiribati),<ref name="kiribaticonst" /> and they must maintain the confidence of the legislature to remain in office.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Siaroff |first=Alan |date=May 2003 |title=Comparative presidencies: The inadequacy of the presidential, semi-presidential and parliamentary distinction |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/european-journal-of-political-research/article/abs/comparative-presidencies-the-inadequacy-of-the-presidential-semipresidential-and-parliamentary-distinction/D284E734414B2B0FF5B6CB1A42AE17D6 |journal=European Journal of Political Research |language=en |volume=42 |issue=3 |pages=287–312 |doi=10.1111/1475-6765.00084 |issn=0304-4130|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Electoral Knowledgebase - Kiribati |url=https://www.electoralknowledgebase.com/countries/oceania/kiribati |access-date=2026-10-19 |website=www.electoralknowledgebase.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Often this form is not considered parliamentary, but a hybrid system.<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Shugart|first=Matthew Søberg|date=2005-12-01|title=Semi-Presidential Systems: Dual Executive And Mixed Authority Patterns|url=https://doi.org/10.1057/palgrave.fp.8200087|journal=French Politics|language=en|volume=3|issue=3|pages=323–351|doi=10.1057/palgrave.fp.8200087|issn=1476-3427}}</ref> This system is more common on subnational levels, such as regions and municipalities, for example Italy uses such a system on both these levels.
{{colbegin|colwidth=40em}}
