*{{flag|Kiribati}}<ref name=kiribaticonst>{{cite web |title=Kiribati's Constitution of 1979 with Amendments through 1995 |url=https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Kiribati_1995.pdf?lang=en |website=www.constituteproject.org |access-date=12 January 2020 |archive-date=11 January 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200111194822/https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Kiribati_1995.pdf?lang=en |url-status=live}}</ref>{{efn|content=Per the Constitution, Kiribati's president is elected by plurality voting after candidates for the presidency are nominated by the newly elected legislature. If a vote of no confidence against the president is successful, they are removed from office and the legislature stands dissolved (triggering a new election for it) in the interim a body known as the "Council of State" (comprising the chief justice, the president of the public service commission and speaker of the legislature) serves the functions of the presidency.}}
{{colend}}

==Presidential systems==
{{main|Presidential system}}
<noinclude><!-- The content of this section is transcluded at Presidential system#States with a presidential system of government -->
In [[presidential system]], a [[President (government title)|president]] is both the [[head of state]] and [[head of government]], and is elected and remains in office independently of the legislature. There is generally no prime minister, although if one exists, in most cases they serve purely at the discretion of the president.</noinclude>

