===Assembly-independent republic===
In an assembly-independent republic, the executive is elected by the legislature but, once in office, is not politically accountable to a majority in the legislature. That is, the executive cannot be removed by a simple-majority vote of the legislature, such as a [[vote of no confidence]].<ref name=semi>{{cite journal |last1=Shugart |first1=Matthew Søberg |title=Semi-Presidential Systems: Dual Executive And Mixed Authority Patterns |journal=French Politics |date=December 2005 |volume=3 |issue=3 |pages=323–351 |doi=10.1057/palgrave.fp.8200087|doi-access=free}}</ref> This places such systems outside the logic of [[parliamentary republic]]s, in which the executive depends on continued legislative confidence, and [[presidential system]]s, in which the executive is elected independently of the legislature. In comparative politics, this institutional arrangement is commonly described as ''assembly-independent government'', referring to systems that combine legislative election of the executive with its independence from parliamentary dismissal.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Ganghof |first=Steffen |title=A new political system model: Semi-parliamentary government |journal=European Journal of Political Research |year=2017 |pages=261–281 |doi=10.1111/1475-6765.12224}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Shugart |first1=Matthew Søberg |last2=Carey |first2=John M. |title=Presidents and Assemblies: Constitutional Design and Electoral Dynamics |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=1992 |page=26}}</ref><ref name=semi/> They may or may not also hold a seat in the legislature.

